body.proof_tex:
Fix \(e=(u,c,c')\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\), and write
\[
\sigma_{c,1}=1,\qquad \sigma_{c,0}=-1,\qquad
\sigma_{c',1}=-1,\qquad \sigma_{c',0}=1.
\]
The four pairs indexed by \((a,s)\) below are \((c,1),(c,0),(c',1),(c',0)\).  By the definitions of \(p_{a,s}\) and \(m_{a,s,u}\), positivity of the candidate propensity, and iterated conditioning, for each of those pairs,
\[
\begin{aligned}
&E_P\!\left[
 \frac{\mathbf 1_{a,s}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
 \{\Delta Y_u-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \mathrel{\Big|}X\right]\\
&\qquad=
\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
\{m_{a,s,u}(X)-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
\]
The candidate restriction level appearing in \(S_e\) is
\(\widetilde g_e(X)=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\), whereas
\(g_e(X)=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}m_{a,s,u}(X)\).  Summing (1) with the four signs and adding \(\widetilde g_e-g_e\) therefore gives the exact identity
\[
\begin{aligned}
E_P[S_e(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e(X)
&=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}
 \{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \left\{1-\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}\right\}\\
&=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}
 \{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \frac{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)-p_{a,s}(X)}
      {\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]
Thus every summand is the product of a propensity error and a regression error.  Along any differentiable one-dimensional path through the truth \(\widetilde\eta=\eta=(p,m)\), both errors vanish at path value zero, so differentiating their product at zero gives zero.  This proves the asserted Gateaux orthogonality in either nuisance coordinate.

The clipping premise and Assumption \(\ref{ass:cell-overlap}\) give
\(1/\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)\le 2/\varepsilon\) almost surely.  Applying the triangle inequality to (2), then H\"older's inequality coordinatewise, yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
\|E_P[S_e(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e(X)\|_{P_X,2}
&\le \frac{2}{\varepsilon}
 \sum_{(a,s)}
 \|\widetilde p_{a,s}-p_{a,s}\|_{P_X,4}
 \|\widetilde m_{a,s,u}-m_{a,s,u}\|_{P_X,4}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]
Because \(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\) and all cell and period index sets are finite, summing (3) over the retained coordinates and using equivalence of finite-dimensional Euclidean norms gives
\[
\|E_P[S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g(X)\|_{P_X,2}
\le C_\varepsilon
\|\widetilde p-p\|_{P_X,4}
\|\widetilde m-m\|_{P_X,4},
\tag{4}
\]
where \(C_\varepsilon<\infty\) depends only on \(\varepsilon\) and the fixed index sets.

Finally, Definition \(\ref{def:orthogonal-complete-normal-signal}\) gives, at the truth,
\[
S_e(O;\eta)=g_e(X)+Z^{\mathrm{per}}_{e,P}(O).
\tag{5}
\]
Every row \(e\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\) is one of the localized observable equality rows.  Hence Theorem \(\ref{thm:observable-equivalence}\), equivalently Definition \(\ref{def:local-observed-model}\), implies \(g_e(X)=0\) \(P\)-almost surely.  Equation (5) then gives
\(S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\eta)=Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}(O)\) almost surely, as claimed.
